<metadata><name>The Spanish Civil War 1936–1939 — DZ Charlie</name><desc>Waypoints from the DZ Charlie field guides. Pins marked LOW or AREA are the centre of a search area, not an exact spot.</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/"><text>DZ Charlie</text></link></metadata>
<wpt lat="40.445200" lon="-3.726700"><name>DZC Ciudad Universitaria front</name><desc>Madrid (Moncloa-Aravaca) · 15 Nov 1936–28 Mar 1939

In mid-November 1936 Nationalist columns under Varela and Asensio, spearheaded by Moroccan Regulares and the Legion, crossed the Manzanares and broke into the half-built University City. The XI and XII International Brigades and the anarchist Durruti column fought them building by building: the School of Architecture fell first, the Hospital Clínico became a Nationalist bastion, the Faculty of Philosophy a Republican strongpoint. Durruti was mortally wounded here on 19–20 November. The line then froze for over two years, with sniping, shelling and mine warfare under the faculties, until Madrid surrendered on 28 March 1939. The salient marks the closest Franco's army came to taking the capital by storm.

A modern campus rebuilt after the war; a few façades show repaired damage, and a memorial to the International Brigades (2011) stands on the campus. The Hospital Clínico San Carlos occupies the building fought over in 1936.

Best walked from Metro Ciudad Universitaria or Moncloa, combined with the Parque del Oeste bunkers and Casa de Velázquez (about 2–3 hours on foot). Campus buildings are working faculties; view from outside.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Battlefields</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="39.858080" lon="-4.020630"><name>DZC Alcázar of Toledo &amp; Army Museum</name><desc>Toledo · 21 Jul–27 Sep 1936

When the coup failed in Toledo, Colonel José Moscardó shut himself in the Alcázar with Civil Guards, cadets, Falangists and their families, plus a number of left-wing hostages. Republican militias besieged the fortress for 70 days, shelling and mining it until the east side collapsed. On 27 September Varela's Army of Africa relieved it, after Franco had diverted his march on Madrid to do so; the capture of Toledo was followed by killings in the town, including of wounded militiamen in the hospital. The siege became the founding legend of Francoism and the Alcázar was rebuilt as a shrine. Since 2010 it houses the Museo del Ejército.

The rebuilt Alcázar, with the Army Museum's Spanish military history galleries; Moscardó's office is preserved and scale models show the fortress before and after the siege.

Museo del Ejército opens Tuesday–Sunday (closed Mondays); allow at least 2 hours. Paid entry, with free periods; check the museum site before travelling.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

