<wpt lat="41.136970" lon="0.492630"><name>DZC Les Devees trenches, La Fatarella</name><desc>La Fatarella · Jul–Nov 1938

The Serra de la Fatarella was the northern shoulder of the Republican Ebro bridgehead and its last stronghold. Les Devees was a rear position guarding the approach to La Fatarella. Soldiers built two shelters here, one in an old lime kiln, joined to a zig-zag trench on higher ground. In mid-November 1938 the final Nationalist offensive broke through at La Fatarella. The last Republican units recrossed the river at Flix on 16 November, ending the battle. The La Fatarella interpretation centre 'Internacionals a l'Ebre' tells the story of the foreign volunteers.

Two restored shelters, one built into a lime kiln, and about 20 m of zig-zag trench, with information panels.

Signposted from La Fatarella; free, open-air site. A 12 km circular route (Ruta de Sant Bertomeu) links it with the Camposines memorial.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Trenches &amp; fortifications</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="41.116230" lon="0.536770"><name>DZC Memorial de les Camposines</name><desc>La Fatarella / Corbera d'Ebre · Jul–Nov 1938 (memorial 2009)

The Camposines crossroads, on the road from the Ebro crossings at Ascó and Flix towards Gandesa, was a key junction behind the Republican front and was fought over in the final weeks of the battle. In 2009 the Generalitat built a memorial here over the line of an old trench. It is an ossuary for the remains of soldiers still being found across the battlefield, from both armies, whose identities are mostly unknown. It is the main place of remembrance for the tens of thousands who died in the battle.

A modern memorial building with an ossuary, beside the hermitage of Sant Bartomeu de les Camposines, and a panoramic view towards the Ebro valley.

Beside the C-43 road junction between Ascó and Corbera d'Ebre; roadside parking; free.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Memorial</sym><type>Memorials</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="41.121740" lon="0.410450"><name>DZC Vilalba dels Arcs: Els Barrancs trenches &amp; Mirador del Bassot</name><desc>Vilalba dels Arcs · 25 Jul–Nov 1938

Vilalba dels Arcs lay on the northern edge of the Republican advance of 25 July 1938. Republican attacks failed to take the village and its hills. The Mirador del Bassot looks out from the defensive positions of the first days over the ground where those assaults were made. Just outside the village, the Els Barrancs site keeps about 700 m of trench line with firing pits, shelters and huts, the best-preserved trench network in the Ebro heritage network. The 'Soldats a les trinxeres' interpretation centre in the village explains the everyday life of the soldiers at the front.

About 700 m of restored trenches, firing pits, shelters and huts at Els Barrancs; a viewpoint with panels in the village.

The 'Soldats a les Trinxeres' interpretation centre in the village is temporarily closed; the Barrancs trenches and the Mirador del Bassot can still be reached on foot or by car. Check with the Espais de la Batalla de l'Ebre network.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

