<wpt lat="42.426800" lon="3.163230"><name>DZC Portbou – Walter Benjamin memorial 'Passatges'</name><desc>Portbou · 26 Sep 1940

Portbou, the railway frontier town, was one of the main crossing points of the Retirada in February 1939. Eighteen months later the flow ran the other way: refugees from Nazi-occupied France tried to reach Spain and Portugal. On 26 September 1940 the German-Jewish philosopher Walter Benjamin, turned back by the Spanish authorities after crossing the Pyrenees on foot, took his own life here. Dani Karavan's memorial 'Passatges' (1994) is a steel corridor descending towards the sea. It belongs to the story of exile rather than to the Civil War itself.

Karavan's steel stairway descending towards the sea beside the cemetery, Benjamin's memorial stone, and the view over the bay.

Free; a short walk up from the station to the cemetery; combine with the border crossing to Cerbère.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

