<wpt lat="43.462350" lon="-3.805620"><name>DZC Santander air-raid shelter (Refugio Antiaéreo)</name><desc>Santander · 1937

Santander, then in the Republican zone, was bombed repeatedly during 1937, and over a hundred shelters were dug beneath the city. This one, under the Plaza del Príncipe (then Plaza de Mariana Pineda), was built in spring 1937. It has three connected galleries for about 70 people, mainly military commanders and city officials. It was abandoned after the war and buried during the rebuilding that followed the great fire of 1941. It was rediscovered in 2006 during works on the Plaza Porticada and opened as a museum in 2014. The city fell to Nationalist and Italian troops on 26 August 1937.

About 100 m of restored underground galleries with audiovisuals, period photographs and objects, a Condor Legion airman's equipment and a 250 kg bomb.

Guided visits only, in small groups (about €3). Closed Mondays; book ahead by phone (942 203 099).

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Air-raid shelters</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="43.382220" lon="-4.852220"><name>DZC El Mazuco &amp; Sierra del Cuera</name><desc>Llanes · 6–22 Sep 1937

After Santander fell, the Nationalists pushed west into Asturias. At the El Mazuco pass and on the limestone heights of the Sierra del Cuera, about 5,000 Republican troops of the XIV Army Corps, many of them CNT militia, held off Navarrese brigades under José Solchaga. Those brigades were far stronger, at about 33,000 men, and were supported by the Condor Legion, Italian units and naval gunfire. The Condor Legion used some of its first carpet-bombing tactics here, but the rugged terrain blunted their effect. Peña Turbina, the highest point of the range, was the last main Republican position; it fell on 22 September, opening eastern Asturias.

A small mountain village below rocky heights. Hiking routes such as Alto de la Tornería–Los Resquilones cross the former front line, where informal traces of positions remain.

About 16 km south of Llanes on the AS-115. Stout boots are needed for the karst hillsides; the bar in El Mazucu is the only service.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Battlefields</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="43.256610" lon="-2.798400"><name>DZC Gaztelumendi breach – Larrabetzu Iron Belt bunker</name><desc>Larrabetzu · 12 Jun 1937

The Iron Belt was built from late 1936 as a ring of trenches, bunkers and gun positions around Bilbao. It was planned for about 70,000 defenders but held by roughly 30,000, and the engineer Alejandro Goicoechea defected to the Nationalists with its plans, revealing its weak points. On 12 June 1937 the Nationalists concentrated heavy artillery and more than a hundred aircraft on the weak Gaztelumendi sector at Larrabetzu and broke through. A last Basque counter-attack at Mantuliz (Derio) failed, and Bilbao fell a week later. Near Sarrikola a concrete bunker was built into a farmhouse; ongoing excavations here have uncovered more remains of the line.

A Civil War bunker built inside a building in the Sarrikola area, near the Gaztelumendi breach sector.

In the rural Sarrikola neighbourhood of Larrabetzu. Treat it as private property and look from the lane; combine it with the Zamudio Iron Belt trail on the same ridge.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Private land — view from the road.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Trenches &amp; fortifications</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="43.285830" lon="-2.870000"><name>DZC Iron Belt trail, Zamudio (Berreaga–Gaztelumendi ridge)</name><desc>Zamudio · 11–13 Jun 1937

Zamudio lies directly below the stretch of the Iron Belt where the Nationalists broke through. The fighting of 11–13 June 1937 spread along the Berreaga–Urrusti–Gaztelumendi ridge, and on 12 June the attackers crossed the line and came down into Zamudio. A signposted 11.1 km memory trail, marked with support from Gogora (the Basque Institute for Memory, Coexistence and Human Rights), climbs to the ridge past surviving trenches and machine-gun nests. It is one of the best places to understand what the Iron Belt actually looked like on the ground, and why it failed against massed artillery and aircraft.

Original trenches and concrete machine-gun posts along the ridge, with explanatory panels and waymarks.

Start from the panel at the Arroeta car park in Zamudio. The 11 km loop takes about 4 h and is hilly; wear walking boots.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Trenches &amp; fortifications</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="43.041640" lon="-2.655140"><name>DZC Otxandio – Andikona bombing memorial</name><desc>Otxandio · 22 Jul 1936

On 22 July 1936, four days after the coup, two Breguet XIX light bombers flown from Recajo (La Rioja) by Ángel Salas Larrazábal and José Muñoz Jiménez bombed and machine-gunned the Andikona square in Otxandio. The square was crowded for the Santa Marina fiestas. About 61 people were killed, most of them civilians and many of them children. Otxandio sat on the front between Biscay and Álava. The raid is often called 'the first Gernika', because it was one of the first air attacks of the war to kill a crowd of civilians. A sculpture in the square honours the victims.

The village square (Andikona) with a memorial sculpture to the victims of the bombing.

A small village on the BI-623 between Durango and Vitoria; the square is in the centre, so parking and a 20-minute visit are easy.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

