<wpt lat="36.740350" lon="-4.098950"><name>DZC Parque de la Memoria 'Málaga 1937', Torre del Mar</name><desc>Torre del Mar (Vélez-Málaga) · Feb 1937

Torre del Mar, about 30 km east of Málaga, was where refugees fleeing along the coast road met those coming down from the villages of the Axarquía during the exodus of February 1937. In 2007 the town opened this memorial garden by the artist Rogelio López Cuenca and architect Santiago Cirugeda, deliberately not a monument but a place to meet. It was among the first sites declared a Place of Memory in Andalusia (2013), and the annual commemoration of the Desbandá has been held here since 2005. Nearby, in Río Seco, a small square is named after Norman Bethune.

A plaque reading 'MÁLAGA 1937 / NUNCA MÁS' split by a crack tracing the coast to Almería, almond trees that flower each February, and more than 400 plaques with survivors' names written in their own hand.

Open access, next to the bus station and the Mancomunidad de la Axarquía building in Torre del Mar. Combine with the Peñón del Cuervo memorial on the way from Málaga.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Memorial</sym><type>Memorials</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="36.706510" lon="-4.452170"><name>DZC Panteón de la Memoria, Cementerio de San Rafael, Málaga</name><desc>Málaga · 1937–1950s; exhumed 2006–2009

After Málaga fell in February 1937, military courts and summary shootings killed thousands of Republican supporters, many against the wall of the old San Rafael cemetery. Their bodies were thrown into mass graves there. Between 2006 and 2009 archaeologists exhumed 2,840 bodies — one of the largest Civil War grave excavations in Spain. The cemetery has since been turned into a park and the remains placed in a memorial pantheon, inaugurated on 11 January 2014. Málaga had also seen heavy repression in the Republican zone in the first months of the war, before the city fell; the two waves of killing should be understood together.

A park on the site of the former cemetery with the Panteón de la Memoria, a monument holding the recovered remains, and walls of victims' names.

Camino de San Rafael, in the Carretera de Cádiz district west of the centre; open during park hours, free.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

