<wpt lat="37.418850" lon="-5.980840"><name>DZC Cementerio de San Fernando — Pico Reja mass grave, Seville</name><desc>Seville · Jul 1936 onwards

General Gonzalo Queipo de Llano seized Seville on 18 July 1936, crushing working-class resistance in Triana and La Macarena and broadcasting nightly radio speeches from Unión Radio that threatened opponents with death. The repression that followed killed thousands in the city. Many were buried in Pico Reja, a mass grave in the municipal cemetery of San Fernando. Among them is probably Blas Infante, the 'father of Andalusian nationalism', shot on 11 August 1936 at km 4 of the Carmona road. Excavations begun in 2020 are one of the largest in Spain.

The municipal cemetery with the excavated Pico Reja area and memorial markers; the exhumation site itself may be fenced.

Cemetery open daily; ask at the entrance for the Pico Reja area. For Infante, his house in Coria del Río is now the Museum of Andalusian Autonomy.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Memorial</sym><type>Repression &amp; memory sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="38.322500" lon="-4.628600"><name>DZC Refugio antiaéreo de Villanueva de Córdoba</name><desc>Villanueva de Córdoba · 1937–1939

Villanueva de Córdoba lay in the Republican-held north of Córdoba province, close to the front around Pozoblanco. In March–April 1937 Queipo de Llano's forces tried to break through to the Almadén mercury mines and were stopped at Pozoblanco by Republican forces under Joaquín Pérez Salas and others; the front then stayed close by until the end of the war. Nationalist aircraft bombed the towns of the area, and in 1937 Villanueva built an underground shelter beneath its main square. It is one of the declared Places of Democratic Memory in Andalusia.

An underground shelter beneath the Plaza de España.

Visits by arrangement; ask at the town hall or tourist office in the Plaza de España.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Flag, Red</sym><type>Air-raid shelters</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="37.679030" lon="-4.548620"><name>DZC Espejo — site of Capa's 'Falling Soldier'</name><desc>Espejo · Early Sep 1936

Robert Capa's photograph of a militiaman falling backwards, published in Vu on 23 September 1936 and in Life in 1937, became the defining image of the war. For decades it was said to show the anarchist Federico Borrell García killed at Cerro Muriano, north of Córdoba, on 5 September 1936. In 2009 José Manuel Susperregui matched the skyline to the outskirts of Espejo, about 50 km away, where the front was quiet at the time; many now think the picture was staged or taken during an exercise. Espejo shows how the Córdoba front lines ran through open countryside in the first summer.

The open, rolling farmland around Espejo, whose skyline was matched to the photographs; no memorial or marker is known.

Drive around the outskirts of Espejo with a copy of the photograph; the fields are private farmland, so view from tracks.

Accuracy: LOW — general area only.

Private land — view from the road.

