<wpt lat="42.307450" lon="-8.628570"><name>DZC Isla de San Simón (concentration camp), Ría de Vigo</name><desc>Redondela · 1936–1943

Galicia fell to the coup within days in July 1936, and the rearguard repression was severe. The small island of San Simón, a former lazaretto in the Ría de Vigo, became a concentration camp and penal colony for political prisoners — first from Vigo and Pontevedra, later from across Spain, including many older prisoners. Overcrowding, hunger and disease killed hundreds (around 250 in 1941 alone), and some inmates were taken out and shot. Declared a Bien de Interés Cultural in 1999, the island is now a cultural centre dedicated partly to memory.

The restored island buildings, bridge to San Antón islet, and memorial and literary route; guided tours explain the camp period.

Visits by written request to the Xunta de Galicia; groups must arrange their own boat. About 2 hours.

Accuracy: HIGH — exact spot known.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

DZ Charlie · dzcharlie.com</desc><link href="https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/"><text>Open the guide</text></link><sym>Memorial</sym><type>Repression &amp; memory sites</type></wpt>
<wpt lat="42.304470" lon="-3.607340"><name>DZC San Pedro de Cardeña monastery (International Brigades camp)</name><desc>Castrillo del Val / Burgos · Dec 1936–Nov 1939

The ancient monastery near Burgos, traditionally linked to El Cid, was turned into a concentration camp in December 1936. From April 1938 it was the main camp for captured International Brigaders, held alongside Spanish prisoners, until it closed in November 1939. British, Irish, American and other volunteers taken at Jarama, Belchite, Calaceite and Gandesa passed through; some were exchanged, others transferred to prisons. Army psychiatrist Antonio Vallejo-Nágera carried out pseudo-scientific studies on inmates here aiming to identify a 'Marxist' psychological type. Memoirs by former prisoners describe hunger, beatings and forced saluting.

The restored monastery church and cloister, today home to a Cistercian (Trappist) community; there is no major memorial to the prisoners.

10 km southeast of Burgos; visits are guided by a monk at set times — check monastery hours. Please be discreet: it is an active monastery.

Accuracy: MEDIUM — right area, exact spot uncertain.

Guide: https://dzcharlie.com/spanish-civil-war/

